  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/25f-perchlike-fishes-6-gobies/papuligobius-uniporus/",
      "html": "<p>Papuligobius uniporus attains a maximum length of about 7.5 cm. The species and the genus were described only in 2003.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "beforeYouBuy",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/25f-perchlike-fishes-6-gobies/papuligobius-uniporus/",
      "html": "<p>This interesting freshwater goby was imported from Vietnam for the first time. There is only one other species in the genus, P. ocellatus, described in 1937, which apparently never appeared in the aquarium hobby. The easiest way to distinguish the two species is the coloration of the caudal fin, which is striped in P. ocellatus, and the coloration of the base of the pectoral fin: P. uniporus has three eye-spots there, P. ocellatus only one.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "waterConditions",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/25f-perchlike-fishes-6-gobies/papuligobius-uniporus/",
      "html": "<p>These gobies have no special demands regarding water chemistry.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/25f-perchlike-fishes-6-gobies/papuligobius-uniporus/",
      "html": "<p>They feed readily on any type of frozen or live food that fits the mouth.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/25f-perchlike-fishes-6-gobies/papuligobius-uniporus/",
      "html": "<p>Against conspecifics P. uniporus can be a bit quarrelsome. The species can change its coloration drastically. Most often they look almost deep black, but they can also show a light stripe or a marbled pattern.</p>"
    }
  ],
